{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011CguHahxBTRQMmdTJUfC27","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The repo is empty, so here's a design sized for a first version. I haven't written anything yet.

## Recommended shape

Generate the PDF on demand in a single request and email it as an attachment, with no queue, no stored files and no background worker.

```
[Export button] ──GET  /api/trips/:id/export.pdf──▶ auth + ownership check
[Email me]      ──POST /api/trips/:id/export/email─▶ load itinerary (Postgres)
                                                     renderItineraryPdf(trip) → Buffer
                                                     ├─ GET:  stream as download
                                                     └─ POST: send via email API to session user's email
```

| Decision | Pick | Why |
|---|---|---|
| PDF rendering | `@react-pdf/renderer` | Pure JS, runs in a normal Node route handler, and the layout is React components. Headless Chrome is heavy and painful on serverless. |
| Email | Resend (Postmark or SES also fine) | One API call with an attachment; a text itinerary is tens of KB. |
| Sync or async | Synchronous | Render plus send should take about a second or two. A queue is only worth adding if you hit timeouts. |
| PDF storage | None | The itinerary in Postgres is the source of truth, so regenerate each time. |
| New tables | One small `itinerary_exports` log (user, trip, status, timestamp) | Gives you rate limiting and a "sent at" record for support. |

The core is one pure function, `renderItineraryPdf(trip) → Buffer`, shared by both routes and easy to test.

## Things to get right from the start

- **Recipient is always the signed-in user's account email.** It is never taken from the request, otherwise the endpoint becomes a spam relay.
- **Ownership check on the trip ID.** Both routes must confirm the trip belongs to the session user.
- **Rate limit the email route**, for example a few per trip per hour, using the log table.
- **Timezones.** Render times in each stop's local zone, not the server's, so store an IANA zone with each itinerary item.
- **Fonts.** react-pdf's built-in fonts are Latin-only, so place names like 東京 need a registered Unicode font (e.g. Noto).
- **Sending domain.** SPF/DKIM verification has DNS lead time, so start it early.

